Once your cards are ready, it's time to bring them to Anki.

!! 1. Running a Sync

# Open Anki.
# Open ''Tools'' ➡ ''TiddlyFlashcards Sync''.

<<readme-img img:"$:/plugins/uzvg/TiddlyFlashcards/images/TiddlyFlashcardsSync.png" caption:"TiddlyFlashcards Sync">>

Or press <kbd>alt+S</kbd>.

!! 2. What a Sync Does

A sync creates notes in Anki for new cards, updates the content of notes that already exist, and applies their suspended state.

Deck and tags are not set in Anki: they come from the tiddler each card belongs to. To change them, change the tiddler and sync again.

Scheduling stays on the Anki side. Cards you have already reviewed keep their review history when their content is updated in place.

!! 3. Sync Is One-way

TiddlyFlashcards syncs from TiddlyWiki to Anki, never the other way around.

TiddlyWiki remains the place where cards are written and managed. Make every change there — edit, delete, suspend or unsuspend — through TiddlyFlashcards. Anki is left to do what it does best: spaced repetition and review.

!! 4. Changes Made in Anki

Everything you do to a managed card in Anki is overwritten at the next sync, including but not limited to:

* Adding cards of the same note types that TiddlyFlashcards uses.
* Editing a card that TiddlyFlashcards manages.
* Deleting a managed card. It is restored at the next sync, but its previous scheduling information is lost.

Deck and tag changes made in Anki are also reset, because both come from the owning tiddler.

!! 5. Checklist

* Save the card in TiddlyWiki before syncing (<kbd>alt+N</kbd>). Unsaved drafts are not synced.
* Make later changes in TiddlyWiki, not in Anki.
* To correct something changed in Anki, make the same correction in TiddlyWiki and sync again.

Next, see what to do when something goes wrong: 👉 <<readme-link index:"5">>.

